# why-review.nuon — who read each `why` against its `use`, and what they read.
#
# Spec: `prds/06-help/01-content-model/prd.md` R5, the clause "`why` only
# where the reason is non-obvious, and never restating what `use` already said".
#
# Why a record and not a check. The restatement is semantic: a competent writer
# restates in different words, so lexical overlap does not see it. Measured
# twice, over the 51 pairs that carry a `why`:
#
#   whole-`why` content-word containment in `use` — corpus mean 0.11, and the
#     known defect `Ctrl+V` scored 0.097, rank 28 of 51, below the mean;
#   best-sentence containment (2026-08-21, finer granularity, to see whether it
#     separated where the whole-text one did not) — corpus mean 0.157, and the
#     known defect `Ctrl+C` scored 0.167 at rank 23 of 51, `Ctrl+Shift+B` 0.143
#     at rank 27. Any threshold that catches them fires on half the manual.
#
# Both proxies are recorded so the next worker does not build a third one. What
# holds the clause instead is a reader — and this file is what stops that being
# a wish. `tests/help-content-model.nu` requires every `why`-carrying entry to
# have a row here whose `digest` matches the exact `use`/`why` pair on disk.
#
# So: **add a `why`, or edit either field, and the gate fails** until someone
# reads the new pair against the restatement rule and records it. That is the
# whole ritual, and it is one line:
#
#   1. read the entry's `use` and `why` together. Does the `why` tell you
#      anything the `use` did not? If not, the fix is to cut it, not to record
#      it. If the `use` is explaining a mechanism, move the mechanism here and
#      leave the gesture there.
#   2. run `nu tests/help-content-model.nu`. It prints the digest to set.
#   3. add or update the row: id, file, digest, reviewer (your session id),
#      date. If you also wrote or revised the pair, record `author` — see
#      below, and expect the gate to refuse the row. `note` is optional and
#      carries anything that qualifies the review.
#
# **`author`, and why it is a field rather than a sentence.** laws.md 2 asks
# for "an independent reviewer … who did not write the claim". Until
# 2026-08-21 that was carried by a prose note reading "reviewer is also the
# author", which the gate ignored: four rows said in plain words that they
# vouched for their own writing and the check passed them. `author` now holds
# the session that wrote or last revised the pair, and
# `tests/help-content-model.nu` fails any row where it equals `reviewer`.
#
# The residual is named rather than dressed up: `author` is optional, because
# inventing one for the rows that predate the field would be the same false
# record pointing the other way, and omitting it is not detectable from the
# data. This hardens an honest record; it does not defeat a careless one.
#
# 2026-08-21 · cc-1787301962 swept all 51 pairs. Four were rewritten in that
# pass and carried the self-review note; the other 47 were written by
# cc-1787250953 / cc-1787256992 / cc-1787260422 and read by someone who did
# not write them.
#
# 2026-08-21 · cc-1787432671 re-read those four — `shell.nuon [Ctrl-T]`,
# `nvim.nuon [<S-Up> …]`, `terminal.nuon [Ctrl+C]`, `terminal.nuon
# [Ctrl+Shift+B]` — having written none of them, and all four stand. Each now
# carries `reviewer: cc-1787432671`, `author: cc-1787301962` and a note giving
# the reading rather than the fact of it, so the judgement can be checked
# instead of trusted. No `use` or `why` text was touched, so every digest in
# this file is unchanged; the reviewers moved, the corpus did not.
#
# 2026-08-21 · `impl-H-1` rewrote the `use` of 14 entries after an independent
# reading against their `source` PRDs and live routes (see `use-review.nuon`,
# which is the record of that clause). Nine of the 14 also carry a `why`, and
# because this digest keys on the `use`/`why` PAIR, all nine rows went stale —
# correctly: a `why` that did not restate the old `use` may well restate the
# new one. `impl-H-1` is the author of the revised pairs and could not sign
# them, so two readers who wrote none of the text did: `impl-H-1-r6` took the
# two `shell.nuon` pairs, `impl-H-1-r7` the four `nvim.nuon`, two
# `terminal.nuon` and one `capsule.nuon` pairs.
#
# Two `why` fields were CUT in that round rather than merely re-read, because a
# reader found them restating their rewritten `use`: `shell.nuon [help]` lost a
# forwarding clause the `use` now carries, and `nvim.nuon [<Esc>]` lost an
# opening that re-said the `use`'s "this does nothing". Both trims were then
# read by a reader who had not made them. That is the ritual working in the
# direction it is supposed to: the fix for a restatement is to cut, not to
# record.

[
    # shell.nuon
    {id: "z <query>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "430480e5111145b8", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "zi", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "da527c275c7d3871", reviewer: "reader-D-1c-spec03", author: "impl-D-1c-spec03", date: "2026-08-21", note: "read the pair fresh, having written neither field. The `use` was rewritten earlier today by a different ticket and already names fzf, the `zoxide query --interactive` shell-out and the exception, so the `why` had a narrow space; it stays inside it. The `use` is gesture plus identification (run it, type to narrow, `Enter`, you land there; the picker is fzf, not tv); the `why` is the grant and its price — a cable file would have to reimplement zoxide's frecency ranking, so fzf is in the required package set as zoxide's dependency and never as a picker to reach for (05-platform/02-package-provisioning/packages-installer/prd.md:36-44, which this near-quotes), and it is not a precedent. `frecency` recurs across the pair, but as a property of the list in the `use` and as the cost of replacing it in the `why`. Checked the author's one deliberate departure from its spec: spec03 step 1 drafted a `why` opening `The picker zi opens is fzf, not television: it shells out to zoxide query --interactive` — which the rewritten `use` now says almost verbatim — and dropping that sentence was correct; keeping it would have been exactly the restatement this file exists to catch. Also confirmed the omission the author flagged: ~/.zoxide.nu:40 puts `--exclude $env.PWD` on `__zoxide_z` (the `z` route) while `__zoxide_zi` at :48 is a bare `zoxide query --interactive`, so 04-shell/prd.md I3 overstates on that one clause and this `why` correctly does not inherit it."}
    {id: "<word>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "9b2306e3c5dee511", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "cd <path>", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "73746044c9149332", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r6", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "re-read after the `use` was rewritten twice. Stands: the `why` carries the one fact the `use` never states — that `cd` is not the builtin but an alias to the `mkcd` wrapper (config.nu:289), the single funnel every kind of navigation converges on — which is what makes the confirm prompt explicable at all. No overlap: the `use` is entirely gesture, the `why` entirely mechanism."}
    {id: "mkcd", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "e835fa7f1d36daa6", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "ls", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "0f7b212163f82343", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "ls -D", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "a67740b44191fb9b", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Ctrl-Space / F1", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "96462df108722934", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Ctrl-T", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "743b915a5df8ceba", reviewer: "cc-1787432671", author: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "re-read by a session that wrote neither field. Stands: the why's first sentence is the shared `_finder_pick_channel` route and the cable-file consequence, which is nowhere in the use. Its second sentence does re-say `inserted at the cursor`, but its payload is the contrast with Ctrl-Space — the use says what the two keys share, the why says what they do not, and that is the pair working as intended rather than one copying the other"}
    {id: "finder", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "9d72b7b3d76f539d", reviewer: "implementer-television-r1", author: "implementer-television", date: "2026-08-23", note: "re-recorded because the `use` was rewritten this session: `{hash, subject}` records from git-log became `{hash}`, per 04-shell/04-television R2(b) bullet 3, which drops `subject` outright. Read by a reader that wrote none of it. The `why` is unchanged and still restates nothing — it names the reason for the typed decode (one opener does the right thing for a file, a directory, a grep hit and a commit) where the `use` names the shapes. Route walked in this tree: finder.nu:135 `git-log => Commits`, finder.nu:232-233 builds `{ hash: ($line | str trim) }` and filters on ^[0-9a-f]{7,}$, no subject constructed anywhere; the single extraction is the channel s, cable/git-log.toml:8. VOUCHED WITH CAVEATS: (a) `the return value is data, not a string` reads as the general case when the four typed shapes are the exceptions — _finder_type returns Any for everything unlisted (finder.nu:137) and the Any arm passes raw strings (finder.nu:238), so env, alias, zoxide, git-files, git-branch, nu-history and channels all come back as plain strings; (b) the ChtSheet `{sheet}` shape (finder.nu:136,235-237) is omitted from both the use and the why, though _finder_open has an arm for it (finder.nu:256-257); (c) R2(b) bullet 4 is user-visible and unmentioned — an empty decode over a non-empty selection now raises (finder.nu:98-100), so a finder call can end in an error rather than []."}
    {id: "tv channel", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "edf405bf05241f37", reviewer: "reader-tv-channel-why", author: "impl-H-3", date: "2026-08-24", note: "re-read after the 06-help/03-browser lane inserted `manual` into the curated list; wrote none of this text, and the diff is exactly one clause (`nu-history` and `manual` (the manual's own fuzzy browser) where the old text had only `and nu-history`) with nothing else in the entry touched. STANDS. Census walked in both directions on this tree: home/dot_config/television/cable/ holds 18 files and the `why` names 18 things — the 17 curated .toml channels plus the `theme` channel, which is theme.toml.tmpl — an exact bijection, nothing named that is absent and nothing shipped that is unnamed; 16 became 17 with manual.toml, so the previous row's count is superseded rather than contradicted. `manual` is described truthfully: cable/manual.toml sources `_help_rows` out of help.nu and previews the full entry (06-help/03 R1, R2), so `the manual's own fuzzy browser` is exactly what it is, and the `why` names the channel by the name it actually ships under, implying nothing about `help`. No restatement, and this edit could not have created one: the `use` names no channel at all, so a census the `use` does not contain cannot be a rephrasing of it — the `use` is what a cable file is and how to add one, the `why` is the invariant, the census, the single exception and the theme rule. Sub-claims re-verified after line drift: `channels` re-sourced per call at finder.nu:167-177 (was :150-157), recent-dirs fed by `_dirstack_list` at cable/recent-dirs.toml:9, the cht -> cht-query two-step at finder.nu:69-86,119, the default ANSI theme at television/config.toml:7, the zi/cdi fzf exception at zoxide.nu:92-93,102. BOTH INHERITED CAVEATS ACCOUNTED FOR. (a) RESOLVED: quicklist is no longer a name without a file — cable/quicklist.toml ships, config.nu:567 sources its runner and :847 binds it to Ctrl-Q, so 04-shell/07 landed and the previous row's flag is retired rather than silently dropped. (b) STILL HOLDS, unchanged in substance: `_finder_pick_channel` still lists `tv list-channels` (finder.nu:167-177) and filters only the literal `channels`, so tv's builtins — `git-diff` among them, which R5's Drop clause names — remain browsable, and `anything else migrates on demand` is true of our cable set and not of what the remote shows. What changed since the last reading is that 04-shell/04-television/prd.md:400-404 now carries that scope correction in the spec, so the gap is documented where it belongs; the clause in the `why` is still the one to sharpen if it is ever reopened, and a reader may not sharpen it. REPORTED, not a defect in this pair: the forced name and its accepted cost — `help` is a clap subcommand of tv, so typing help in the Ctrl-Space remote will never match this channel (06-help/03 R1, restated in cable/manual.toml's header) — appears nowhere in the manual, because the `help --fuzzy` entry carries no `why` at all; that is the one non-obvious reason a reader would want here and cannot get, and it is the help lane's coverage rather than this entry's. `author` is `impl-H-3`, the id this node's own spec01 section 6 assigns to its implementer: that session stamped no id in its report, so this is the best-supported name rather than a copied one, and the substance the field exists for holds either way — this reader wrote none of the text."}
    {id: "grep", file: "shell.nuon", digest: "b4fccb79b4025b7b", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
