    {id: "<C-v> (visual)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "857dcd997d08cbd6", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <C-n> <C-p> <C-Space> <C-e>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "8ba5e6770c0e113c", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<BS> and <CR> (autopairs)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "79d3149fc3ef4c30", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the `use` states the two observable gestures only (BS deletes a pair as one press, CR splits the bracket unless a menu is open); the `why` supplies the mechanism the `use` never names — blink.cmp's <CR> keymap is {\"accept\", \"fallback\"} (completion.lua:15), fallback runs whatever <CR> mapping existed before blink.cmp claimed the key, and because autopairs.lua sets up before completion.lua in load order that prior mapping is autopairs' own bracket-expand handler, so the expand survives only through the fallback chain and only while no menu is open; <BS> has no such chain, it is the one map nothing else claims. No restatement: the use never mentions blink.cmp, load order or fallback. Measured headless against this repo's own config, with InsertEnter allowed to settle for blink.cmp's async keymap setup: filetype lua, type `local t = {` then <CR> with no menu open -> buffer becomes ['local t = {', '  ', '}'] (bracket still expands), and maparg confirms <BS> -> 'autopairs delete' (buffer=1), <CR> -> 'blink.cmp: Accept' (buffer=1) — matching autopairs.lua's own header comment read correctly (its plain claim that map_cr 'does not survive' is about which desc string wins, not about the behaviour disappearing)."}
    {id: "<leader>e", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "3d35cc67da8009d6", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the old `why` said oil was lazy on <leader>e and that `:e some/dir` reached neither oil nor netrw before that press — backwards. Confirmed `lazy = false` at home/dot_config/nvim/lua/plugins/explorer.lua:38, and measured headless against this repo's own config (XDG_CONFIG_HOME at home/dot_config): `:e <cwd>` with no key pressed gives filetype=oil, bufname=oil:///Users/feb/dev/dotfiles/. So oil's default_file_explorer hijack is live at startup and :e reaches it with no key press; netrw stays disabled either way. The `use` is unchanged and still describes <leader>e correctly as a working fast path."}
    {id: "<leader>cf", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "7755bc6ab92859da", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<leader>t", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "e70cd08965d13ba7", reviewer: "reader-leader-t-why", author: "implementer-markdown-tables", date: "2026-08-23", note: "the `use` asserts only that alignment is already on in a markdown buffer; the `why` supplies the mechanism it never names, and both halves check out. The plugin really does ship inactive and per buffer — `table_mode_always_active` defaults to 0 (plugin/table-mode.vim:33) and `tablemode#IsActive` reads the buffer-local `b:table_mode_active` (autoload/tablemode.vim:146, 192-195) — so the FileType autocmd in table-mode.lua is what switches it on, and the counterfactual is measured, not asserted: tests/nvim-markdown-tables.sh cf2 deletes the autocmd and gets `b2_active=nil` on `:edit t2.md` while the first buffer stays `active=1`. This replaces the false reason the previous row (impl-H-1-r7, digest 3990fd6e3dddb2d7) vouched for after reading the live config's comment rather than measuring it, so the correction is real and the digest move is earned. One imprecision recorded rather than fixed, since a reader may not edit the text: `the file you opened nvim with` is exact for the fixture this was measured on (`nvim t.md`), but what the redundant direct `enable()` covers is whichever buffer was current at plugin load — start bare and `:e a.md` and that first `:edit` aligns too, so the counterfactual names a case, not the rule. Also noted as thin rather than wrong: nothing here carries the reason most specific to this entry's own key, that the `<leader>t` prefix is set in `init` because plugin/table-mode.vim:48-58 derives nine map names from it at load time, and that the value buys no behaviour since it is already the plugin default."}
    {id: "<leader>ff and <leader><space>", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "078b4f8e3e835629", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "<Tab> <S-Tab> <CR> (telescope)", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "88bce566500afd15", reviewer: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps-r1", author: "implementer-nvim-help-entry-gaps", date: "2026-08-25", note: "the `why`'s old claim 'there is no lhs to introspect' is refuted, not merely superseded: measured headless against this repo's own config (XDG_CONFIG_HOME at home/dot_config), with a live `Telescope find_files` picker open, `vim.fn.maparg('<Tab>', 'i', false, true)` returns a full table with `lhs = '<Tab>'` and `buffer = 1` — an lhs plainly is there to introspect. `kind: prose` still stands, on the real constraint: telescope.lua:41-58 sets these three keys inside `telescope.setup({ defaults = { mappings = ... } })`, which telescope applies as a buffer-local map on the prompt buffer it creates when a picker opens, not at config-load time and not on any buffer before or after. So maparg sees the map only from inside a live picker on that picker's own buffer; no static/headless-with-no-picker probe has anything to check against. The `use` is unchanged and still matches (typing-or-normal-mode, worse/better direction)."}
    {id: "gd and gI", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "f6b0881d074e0d31", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Neovim's own LSP keys", file: "nvim.nuon", digest: "8a795c015b386192", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r7", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the `use` was rewritten this session (]d/[d open no float on 0.12; <C-w>d is the key that does); the `why` was not. Stands: the `use` lists the keys, the `why` answers the question it raises but cannot address — why keys this config does not own appear in a manual of this config, and that they are the single stated exception to the allowlist rule. Both checked: ~/.config/nvim/lua/plugins/lsp.lua:21-33 adds only gd, gI, <leader>rn, <leader>ca and re-maps none of grn/gra/grr/gri/gO/K/]d/[d; the exception is real at prds/06-help/04-drift-check/prd.md:42-44. One imprecision left standing, below the refusal line: `from 0.11 onward` is right for grn/gra/grr/gri/gO, while K, ]d, [d and <C-w>d were defaults from 0.10 — under-dated rather than false."}

    # terminal.nuon
    {id: "F5 <digit>", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "b255cc072dae0a91", reviewer: "implementer-f5-jump-r1", author: "implementer-f5-jump", date: "2026-08-22", note: "why rewritten, use untouched; read the pair fresh against the landed config. Every claim traced: one_shot+until_unknown+timeout_milliseconds=JUMP_TIMEOUT_MS at wezterm.lua:712-717 (:325), digits over TAB_COUNT -> ActivateTab (:338-344), all 26 letters -> act.Nop (:359-365), format-tab-title prints the digit and nothing else (:506-508) with the clock corner unconditional (:638-643); `tried and removed as noise` is R3's own history (prd.md:52-60) and the letters-as-bare-cancel chain is R2 in substance (prd.md:43-51). No restatement: the use is the digits/Esc/second-key gesture, the why adds the mechanism name, the unmapped-key exit (`mapped or not` — new), the legend history and the letter binding; its opening `not a mode you have to leave` sits nearest the use's `over the moment you press the second key` but the payload is new — borderline the way help --check's row is, left standing. The timeout exit is in neither field; that qualifier lives on this entry's use-review row (use-review.nuon:193), not here."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+<arrow>", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "dbf0276b17641617", reviewer: "implementer-f5-jump-r1", author: "implementer-f5-jump", date: "2026-08-22", note: "read the pair fresh, having written neither field. The use is gesture and effect only; the why is provenance, history and constraint — defaults deliberately left unshadowed (R4, prd.md:61-66), the pane half dropped by Q2 answered 2026-08-21 (prd.md:19-28), the modal ban — none of which the use touches, so no restatement. `a modal opened from Lua can never be closed from Lua` checks against epic I3 (02-terminal/prd.md:53-62): cancel_modal reachable from no KeyAssignment, PaneSelector::perform_assignment returns false unconditionally; R6 (prd.md:77-81) says the same. Qualifier left standing: I3/R6 give TWO reasons the modal is out — a table-bound key is consumed before the modal ever sees it, AND nothing in Lua closes it — and this why carries only the second; partial, not false."}
    {id: "nine tabs", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "b3db4cf38ed1176a", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "lit and dim tabs", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "3bbefb68ebf1f401", reviewer: "implementer-tab-content-state-r1", author: "implementer-tab-content-state", date: "2026-08-23", note: "the `use` says what you see and when; the `why` says what is being read, why it is learned rather than named, and why the tick is the mechanism — four claims, each checked against its own source, none of them a restatement. (1) OSC 133 marking is deliberately off: prds/04-shell/01-core-config R5, and the block's own comment carries the reason (double-marks starship's two-line prompt under WezTerm, phantom blank line). The `use` never mentions prompts at all, so this is a cause and not a rephrasing. (2) The learned baseline is this node's R4 (prds/02-terminal/05-tab-content-state/prd.md:44-54): the classification must not require the shell to cooperate, and the block holds no shell name — verified, 0 hits for a quoted nu/zsh/bash/fish/sh inside it. (3) `emits no event for a pane opening or closing` is the tab reconciler's own recorded finding in wezterm.lua (:29-35 for tab close, :300-311 for the trigger list) and the same fact restated in this block's comment (:808-814); it is what makes the five seconds a mechanism rather than a delay, and it is also R6's stale-state decay. (4) The two-channel split is this node's R3 third clause, and in code it is the tab.is_active early return: background carries focus, foreground carries occupancy. Qualifier recorded rather than hidden: the `use` asserts the timing and the `why` explains it, so `five seconds` appears in both — the shared token is the observable in one and its cause in the other, which is the split this check is for, but it is the one place a reader could call restatement."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+X", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "7adce967752bbe1d", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `why` is untouched; the `use` was revised this session to say where search is, so the pair re-digests and the pair is what gets re-read. It stands. The `why` is three implementation constraints and the `use` states none of them: reset-on-entry, which is copy_selecting cleared then ClearSelection then ActivateCopyMode in that order (wezterm.lua:385-389); pane-id tracking with the blank-cell reason (wezterm.lua:374-380); and extended-not-replaced, which is default_key_tables().copy_mode plus one table.insert (wezterm.lua:419-420). The revised sentence in the `use` is a pointer to another key, and the `why` says nothing about search, so the edit cannot have created a restatement."}
    {id: "Ctrl+V", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "0ef88c8a069e13be", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Ctrl+C", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "8823768355048264", reviewer: "cc-1787432671", author: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "re-read by a session that wrote neither field, and this is the pair a second reader caught once already. Stands: the use gives two independent cases, the why gives the one ordered test that produces them — selection read first, key sent only on empty — plus the trade a plain CopyTo loses. Neither sentence is the other in different words"}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+D", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "36384356a8515f8b", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+B", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "69bbc04b413e3091", reviewer: "cc-1787432671", author: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "re-read by a session that wrote neither field. Stands, and it is the cleanest of the four: the use is the gesture and when to reach for it, the why is entirely about the key having looked contested and the wallpaper pipeline being DO NOT PORT. No sentence of one appears in the other, and the rebuild rationale now points at the entry that owns it instead of copying it"}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+S", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "e97251406741c281", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r7", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "the `use` was rewritten this session (R2 specifies a fuzzy-selectable list and the old text named no typing step); the `why` was not. Stands: the `why` adds ownership and its consequence — the list is written by the capsule tool on every successful mount rather than by the terminal, hence one list however the container was started, and it outlives a WezTerm restart. Both trace to 01-capsule/04-recent-workspaces/prd.md:20-23 (R1) and :31-32 (R4). Flag outside the clause: this key is unbuilt — `wezterm show-keys --lua` has no S+CTRL binding at all, unlike X — so its wezterm-key target is a contract, not a passing check."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+T", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "be71ca7c4a6f7fc9", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21"}
    {id: "F6", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "9d483752aa0bdb87", reviewer: "implementer-terminal-appearance-r1", author: "implementer-terminal-appearance", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the use asserts two observable facts (works over a TUI; applies everywhere at once); the why supplies their non-obvious causes, which the use does not contain — bound in WezTerm because a shell-level binding is swallowed by a full-screen TUI (wezterm.lua:296-298), and backgrounded because tinty apply's synchronous hook chain would freeze every window (wezterm.lua:300-304). Both are exactly R11's two named constraints (prd.md:150-158); neither sentence restates the gesture or its result."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Shift+F", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "b8aa9c0ae3baf280", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` gives the gesture and the four keys of the search prompt; the `why` gives two facts the gesture cannot show — that the binding is WezTerm's own default the config deliberately leaves unshadowed, and that copy mode has no search at all because the two are separate key tables. Neither is in the `use`, and both are measured: search_mode is 10 rows, the top-level 'F' with 'CTRL' act.Search row is present, and nothing in wezterm.lua binds or shadows either. No restatement."}
    {id: "copymode", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "d486b6f4553ebe20", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` says what typing the command does; the `why` says by what mechanism, which the `use` never mentions: an OSC 1337 user-var parsed off the pty, the only route from a shell command into a GUI-only mode because WezTerm's CLI exposes no copy-mode action. The second sentence carries the refusal — the sibling background-transparency user-var, dropped by prds/00-delivery/decisions/wallpaper-opacity — which is a constraint on the handler rather than anything the gesture could reveal, and it is why the handler at wezterm.lua:398-402 has one arm. No restatement."}
    {id: "Ctrl+Alt+Super+drag", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "9d515dd64e155b1f", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` is the gesture and its result; the `why` is the two reasons behind it, neither stated there: RESIZE decorations leave no titlebar, so this is the only handle for repositioning (config.window_decorations is RESIZE at wezterm.lua:650), and the heavy three-modifier combo is chosen to keep the binding off plain clicks and selection drags. The `use` mentions that ordinary drags still select, which is the observation; the `why` says the modifier weight is what buys it, which is the cause. Distinct claims, no restatement."}
    {id: "Ctrl+click", file: "terminal.nuon", digest: "c554d598d84bd5ab", reviewer: "implementer-copy-mode-r1", author: "implementer-copy-mode", date: "2026-08-22", note: "the `use` claims two observable things — the link opens even under a mouse-capturing TUI, and a plain click still reaches the program. The `why` supplies the single cause the `use` does not name: the mouse_reporting flag on the binding (wezterm.lua:855), without which WezTerm forwards the click to the application and nothing opens the URL. That is the mechanism behind the first claim rather than a restatement of it, and it is R8's own recorded reason."}

    # capsule.nuon
    {id: "capsule [dir]", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "1a796bc7d3e776f9", reviewer: "impl-H-1-r7", author: "impl-H-1", date: "2026-08-21", note: "PRD-only, no live reading: `capsule` is absent from $PATH. The `use` was rewritten this session (zsh, the timing scope, the R7 recency hook); the `why` was not. Stands: it carries two things the `use` never touches — the naming scheme with the short path hash that keeps two `api` directories apart (01-capsule/01-container-lifecycle/prd.md:21-24, R1), and the consolidation history, whose five pieces are verbatim the epic's own list (01-capsule/prd.md:13-17), so count, membership and `overlapped and drifted` are sourced rather than invented. On the past-tense `replaced`, asked about explicitly: neither a restatement nor a truth problem — the half asserted as past (five pieces existed, overlapped, drifted) is historically true per the epic, and the unbuilt half is the command, which this whole corpus deliberately writes in the deployed present as the contract the work must meet. Recorded anyway: `replaced` is the only verb in these seven that reads as a dated event rather than as that contract, so a future tense rule for unbuilt surfaces would catch this sentence first."}
    {id: "capsule --rebuild", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "23fac1c72bfc6fe9", reviewer: "cc-1787301962", date: "2026-08-21", note: "borderline and left standing: `the only way to force a rebuild` restates the use's first clause, but the exclusivity is new and the sentence after it is the constraint"}
    {id: "credentials in a capsule", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "aac5f0c9f429e1e6", reviewer: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy-r1", author: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy", date: "2026-08-25", note: "second reading, distinct from the author, of the trimmed entry against `01-capsule/03-credential-propagation/prd.md` and `setup-credentials.sh`. The SSH sentence (`~/.ssh` mount, key copy, `600/700`, host-key pre-add) is removed here, not deleted from the corpus — it now lives, corrected, in `ssh in a capsule`'s own row below, which is the fix for `capsule-creds-doc-accuracy` findings 1 and 2. Finding 3 (silent on agent auth) is fixed by `agents in a capsule` existing at all rather than by anything added here. What remains — hygiene, `.gitconfig`, the HTTPS refresh block, the directory bind — is unchanged text, already read twice (`df68bce54ec761bf`'s row, retired by this edit, and the two independent readings it records), so this pass checked only that removing the SSH sentence left no dangling reference and that the `use` (byte-identical, still pure outcome: `git pull`, `git push`, `ssh -T`, `claude`, `opencode`, no mechanism named) still contains none of the remaining `why`'s clauses. It does not. 122 words."}
    {id: "ssh in a capsule", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "9df5ab23b15d3813", reviewer: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy-r1", author: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy", date: "2026-08-25", note: "new entry, read against `setup-credentials.sh` by a reader distinct from the author. The `use` is pure outcome (`ssh -T`, `git pull`, `git push`, no host-key prompt) and names no mechanism, so every `why` clause is new information. Checked against the script directly: `install -d -m 700 \"$HOME/.ssh\"` (:59), `install -m 644` for `*.pub` and `install -m 600` for private keys (:67-68) — so `600/700` covers the directory and private keys only, never the public key, which is exactly `capsule-creds-doc-accuracy` finding 1 and is now stated as a distinction rather than a blanket claim. `StrictHostKeyChecking accept-new` (:115) is the no-prompt guarantee, and the comment at :109-114 confirms it is deliberately never `no`, which disables verification rather than only the prompt. `ssh-keyscan -T 5 -t rsa,ecdsa,ed25519 github.com` (:121-130) runs before the guarantee is needed and is guarded by `warn` on failure rather than abort (:127), which is what makes it the optimisation and `accept-new` the backstop — finding 2, now attributed to the right line. Also read against `01-capsule/03-credential-propagation/prd.md` R1, whose 2026-08-23 amendment already independently confirms the same three modes and the same three script lines. 111 words."}
    {id: "agents in a capsule", file: "capsule.nuon", digest: "98f4b460bce52fc0", reviewer: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy-r1", author: "implementer-capsule-creds-doc-accuracy", date: "2026-08-25", note: "new entry, read against `setup-credentials.sh` by a reader distinct from the author. The `use` is pure outcome (`claude`, `opencode`, no login prompt) and names no mechanism, so the `why` is new information throughout. Checked against the script: the Claude credential is symlinked, not copied — `ln -sfn \"$CREDS/claude-credentials.json\" \"$HOME/.claude/.credentials.json\"` (:210), inside the block :208-213 — and OpenCode's is the same pattern at :226, inside :224-229. The reason is the script's own comment at :193-202: a writable copy would let the container run its own OAuth refresh, and Claude's refresh rotates the refresh token, so a self-refreshing capsule would invalidate the host's login; the failure mode (idle capsule, expired token, re-mount or host-side `claude` heals it) is the same sentence the comment gives. This is finding 3 fixed — the entry the manual never had, delivering the `agents` topic's own promise (`topics.nuon`: 'what a capsule hands an agent'). Also read against `01-capsule/03-credential-propagation/prd.md` R4, which gives the identical reasoning and the identical failure mode. 95 words."}
]
